<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="yellow">Delusions of Grandeur</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">(DOGMud)</ansi>

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ The World ━━━</ansi>

You are on <ansi fg="yellow">Gaius</ansi>, a world colonized ten thousand years ago by humans from a distant
civilization. The colony ships crashed. The technology was lost. Survivors scattered
across the continent of <ansi fg="yellow">Thera</ansi> and rebuilt from nothing, eventually reaching a world
that looks, from the outside, like medieval Europe — cities, trade routes, lords and
peasants, swords and shields.

The truth is more complicated.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ The Chrysalis ━━━</ansi>

Something in the air, water, and soil of Gaius infected the survivors. The dominant
religion calls it <ansi fg="yellow">The Chrysalis</ansi> — a divine gift, an awakening. What it actually is
no one fully understands. What it does is undeniable:

The beliefs and convictions of the infected <ansi fg="white">become physically real</ansi>.

Magic is not supernatural. It is the Chrysalis translating will into matter. Mutations
are not random — they reflect the pressures, fears, and drives of the individual the
organism inhabits. A soldier who survives a hundred battles finds his body changing to
match. A healer's hands become steadier than any uninfected surgeon's. The deeply
faithful shape reality around their faith.

Infection manifests at puberty. It grows stronger with age. There is no cure.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ The Three Moons ━━━</ansi>

Three moons hang in the sky of Gaius. The people of Thera study them, worship them,
and build their calendars around them:

  <ansi fg="yellow">Swiftmoon</ansi>    — the large fast moon, linked to dexterity and quick thought
  <ansi fg="yellow">The Wanderer</ansi> — the middle moon, linked to perception and foresight
  <ansi fg="yellow">The Eye</ansi>      — the small bright moon, linked to charisma and force of will

What no living person knows: the moons are not moons. They are the other colony ships,
still in orbit after ten thousand years. Their presence influences the Chrysalis in
ways that look exactly like astrology and feel exactly like divine favour.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ The Windward Marches ━━━</ansi>

The game begins in <ansi fg="yellow">The Windward Marches</ansi>, a region of varied terrain on the western
edge of Thera — forests, cliffs, shore, highland passes. The climate runs cool and
temperate, prone to sudden storms at the seasonal turns. It is a place where old
power is contested and new factions are rising.

<ansi fg="yellow">Startland</ansi> is the entry point: a rough settlement where newcomers find their
footing before venturing further into the world.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ What Kind of Game Is This? ━━━</ansi>

<ansi fg="white">Skill-based.</ansi>  There are no class restrictions. You become what you practice.
  Fight with weapons: weapon combat improves. Cast spells: spellcasting improves.
  Forge iron: blacksmithing improves. Skills advance through use, not through
  spending points at a trainer.

<ansi fg="white">Stat-based.</ansi>  Six stats govern your character: Strength, Dexterity, Vitality,
  Perception, Willpower, Charisma. All start near 100 (human baseline) and rise
  through use. A mage who never lifts a sword will have weak arms. A blacksmith
  who never trains the mind will struggle with spells.

<ansi fg="white">Mutation-driven.</ansi>  Sustained combat feeds the Chrysalis. Your body changes. Every
  mutation has a benefit and a cost. You do not choose them — the organism does.
  You can carry up to five mutations at once.

<ansi fg="white">Crafting.</ansi>  Raw materials become gear through skill and a working station. A forge
  makes blades. An alchemy bench makes potions. What you can craft depends on
  your skill and what you can find or gather.

